--TEST--
`as Markup` trusts only a source literal and `+` composes only two `Markup` — `rule:core-classes/html-auto-escape`'s two bounds
--FILE--
<?nvs
// § 5's first bullet, on both sides of its bound: the literal above is trusted
// because it is exactly what the developer wrote in the file, and the computed
// string below can never become trusted markup this way. That is what closes
// "compute the escape-defeating payload at runtime, then cast it" — a string
// whose bytes were assembled at run time has no author to trust.
Core\Html\Markup $ok = "<b>" as Core\Html\Markup;
string $computed = "<b" . ">";
Core\Html\Markup $bad = $computed as Core\Html\Markup;

// § 5's second bullet reads `Markup` **+** `Markup`, and the emphasis is on
// both sides: `+` composes two already-trusted fragments and is not a sink, so
// it has nowhere to escape a plain string on its way in.
echo $ok + "raw", "\n";
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0417]: only a string written directly in the code can be converted `as Markup`. A computed or `tainted` value cannot become trusted markup this way
%A
error[E0716]: `+` has no meaning for `Core\Html\Markup`
%A
   = help: `+` adds `Markup` only to `Markup`. Convert the other operand with `as Markup` if it is a string written directly in the code, or escape it with `Core\Html::escape(...)`. `+` does not escape it for you

error: aborting due to 2 errors
To read what a code means and how to fix it, run `nvs agent show <code>`.
